Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server that wraps a REST API using HttpClient with stdio transport:

---

## Tool Class — `ApiTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Fetch a user by their ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The numeric user ID to look up")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync($"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Create a new user in the system.")]
    public async Task<string> CreateUser(
        [Description("Display name for the new user")] string name,
        [Description("Email address")] string email,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var payload = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { name, email });
        var content = new StringContent(payload, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");
        var response = await httpClient.PostAsync("/users", content, cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

## `Program.cs` — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for your API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK resolves dependencies **automatically** — no manual wiring needed:

| Pattern | How it works |
|---------|--------------|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class) | The class is instantiated per-request. DI services go in the primary constructor (like `HttpClient` and `ILogger<T>` above). |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | DI services can appear directly as method parameters. The SDK sees they're registered in DI and injects them — they **do not** appear in the tool's JSON schema sent to the LLM. |

**Key rules:**
- `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>`, and any DI-registered service are **never** exposed in the schema
- Only parameters with `[Description]` become LLM-visible tool inputs
- Use `AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(...)` for typed `HttpClient` injection with a configured base address

Run with:
```bash
dotnet build && dotnet run
```

The server will block on stdin waiting for JSON-RPC messages from an MCP client (VS Code, GitHub Copilot, MCP Inspector, etc.).
